§§ `DBS Woman's World -- the indicator decides, and the sheet assumed it`

-- Eleven failures, all the same shape, and the last of them is one of the two
-- discrepancies this acceptance test was pre-registered to find.
--   [dbs-womans-card-tnc cl.13] "DBS determines an online transaction based
--     on system indicators. These indicators are decided by the merchant and
--     their banker, and passed to DBS when the transaction is posted to the
--     Card."
-- The indicator is attached when the charge is POSTED, by the merchant's
-- banker, after the cardholder has acted. It is therefore not a fact any
-- fixture may carry, every fixture in cheatsheet-claims.l4 sets it `Unknown`,
-- and `miles per dollar` reports the LOWER of the two branches so a headline
-- never overstates. The sheet's "4" is the module's
-- `miles per dollar if the merchant flags online`, which is 4 on every row
-- here; the headline is cl.15's base 1X, 0.4 miles per dollar.
--
-- Fork-register entry: `F-womans-online-definition`, readings
-- `indicatorDecides` (taken) against `viaTheInternetDecides` (rejected). Its
-- own note names this file's last row: "This is the site a secondary source
-- is most likely to get wrong: a household cheat sheet that says an in-store
-- wallet tap 'counts as online' is refuted on cl.11 and cl.13 read together."
--
-- PRE-REGISTERED DISCREPANCY 1 of 2 is the wallet-tap row at the end. It is
-- pinned with a third assertion the other rows do not carry, because the
-- claim it refutes is about the OUTCOME and not about the rate: the sheet
-- says a wallet tap in store counts as online, and the module says the
-- outcome is not determined by how the cardholder paid.

